<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="violdelphin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7458" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The occurrence of 7-polyacylated anthocyanins as the main pigments inducing blue color(s) in flowers has been reported from various plant families |CITS:[19554240]|. Many of them are delphinidin derivatives which have been found in the &lt;i&gt;Campanulaceae&lt;/i&gt; family, for instance |FRAME:CPD-16561| (this pathway) |CITS:[Kondo90] [7763601]|, |FRAME:CPD-16594| |CITS:[7763601] [Justesen97]| and |FRAME:CPD-16596| |CITS:[Saito71] [Goto83]| but also in the &lt;i&gt;Asteracea&lt;/i&gt; family where |FRAME:CPD-16595| dominates |CITS:[Goto84]| and the &lt;i&gt;Goodeniaceae&lt;/i&gt; family with |FRAME:CPD-16605| as the principal blue flower pigment |CITS:[17174991]|. The formation and color depth of the blue hues depend on the developmental stage of the sepals during anthesis |CITS:[12353624]| or the genetic constitution of the plants |CITS:[Justesen97]| which in turn reflects the activation and specificities of the involved enzymes. The formation of |FRAME:CPD-7145| from |FRAME:CPD-7117| |CITS:[Kamsteeg80]| is considered the first committed step in the |FRAME:CPD-16561| biosynthesis followed by the crucial step of the 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosylation of the delphinidin 3-rutinoside to yield |FRAME:CPD-16558| |CITS:[20971893] [22673029]|. The anthocyanin 3,7-diglucosides are also the initial point for the biosynthesis of pigments other than delphinidin derivatives (see |FRAME:PWY-7464|). The noteworthiness of this pathway is the utilization of one acyl-glucose donor, i.e. |FRAME:CPD-15283| by both glucosyltransferases and acetyltransferases which further furnish the delphinidin 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-side chain. The donor provides both glucosyl- and p-hydroxybenzoyl groups and in doing so serves as a zwitter donor in the |FRAME:PWY-7458| |CITS:[24179131]|. The two acylation steps in the pathway, i.e. the conversion of |FRAME:CPD-16558| to |FRAME:CPD-16559| and |FRAME:CPD-16560| to |FRAME:CPD-16561| have been demonstrated in crude extracts from sepals of &lt;i&gt;Delphinium grandiflorum&lt;/i&gt;. Although the cDNA (SCPL2) for the acyl-glucose dependent anthocyanin glucoside(s) acyltransferase was obtained the expression in an recombinant organism failed but its molecular function could be confirmed &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; in white &lt;i&gt;Delphinium&lt;/i&gt; varieties where the gene was absent |CITS:[24179131]|. Two cDNA&apos;s encoding for the acyl-glucose dependent anthocyanin 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-(6-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-(p-hydroxybenzoyl)-glucoside) glucosyltransferase catalyzing the conversion of |FRAME:CPD-16559| to |FRAME:CPD-16560| were also isolated, expressed in &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; and biochemically identified. Both, glucosyltransferase and acyltransferase were localized to the vacuole which coincides with the localization of |FRAME:CPD-16561| |CITS:[24179131]| and the occurrence of other enzymes of the pathway |CITS:[20971893] [22673029]|.</Description>
